---
// Homework-submission instructions for CS 50. Not linked from the site; students got the URL directly.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Card from '../../components/Card.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Codeboard submission"
  description="How CS 50 homework is submitted and graded on Codeboard."
  lead="SMC CS 50 · Introduction to C Programming"
>
  <Card>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        For this week’s assignments and onward, we will be using <a href="https://codeboard.io">codeboard.io</a> for
        homework submissions. This will make submitting homework easier for a number of reasons:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          You can now verify your grade before the deadline. When you hit “Submit” on Codeboard, a testing framework
          will execute and check the output of your program. This makes it easier to get full points, as you know in
          advance which cases your program does not handle correctly.
        </li>
        <li>
          Online IDE means you can work anywhere with an internet connection. There’s no more zipping of files, Visual
          Studio, or XCode projects to set up. I will provide you with starter code, and then you go from there. You
          hit Submit, and you’ve submitted your assignment.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>See a demo video of Codeboard here:</p>
      <div class="video">
        <iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/OqIvmbc6nrg" title="Codeboard demo" allow="fullscreen" allowfullscreen></iframe>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Card>

  <Card label="Notes about Codeboard">
    <ol>
      <li>
        You are free to continue to use Visual Studio or XCode if you wish. However, all grading will occur on
        Codeboard, so you are required to submit and pass the test cases on Codeboard.
      </li>
      <li>
        You <strong>must</strong> be signed in when you submit. If you anonymously submit, you may be given a 0.
        <ul>
          <li>
            Please make an account and use your <strong>SMC username</strong> as your Codeboard account username
            (remove all spaces, underscores, and hyphens). If I cannot identify whose account belongs to whom, you will
            get a 0 until the problem is fixed.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        You <strong>must</strong> save your work under the “<em>Project → Save changes</em>” menu item. <strong>Changes do not save automatically</strong>. <strong>Changes will not save unless you are signed up
        and logged into your Codeboard account</strong> (you can tell whether or not you are signed in from the bottom
        left corner). Your browser should warn you if you attempt to close the tab/page without saving. <strong>Pay attention</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Follow the instructions of each assignment. The output restrictions/expectations for test cases are very
        rigid. A single misplaced character will result in failing a test case (or all).
        <ul>
          <li>
            Any attempt to manipulate or subvert the grader without satisfying the requirements <strong>will result in an automatic 0</strong> for the assignment/quiz.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        When you submit your assignment, your output may look odd. The only thing you need to pay attention to is the
        green or red test case output. The testing framework modifies text input/output (so I can compare against
        correct answers), so it changes how the input/output appears.
      </li>
      <li>
        The following naming conventions are reserved for the testing framework. Should you not adhere to these rules,
        you may get a compilation error. You are <strong>not</strong> permitted to name any function or data member
        (struct, enum, etc.) according to the following:
        <ol>
          <li>
            <code>main()</code> - the testing framework defines <code>main()</code> for you. Your main is called <code>user_main()</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Any function or data type starting with the name <code>reserved_</code>. For instance, <code>reserved_test()</code> is not permitted for use. These are reserved for the testing framework.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      This tool is meant to make your life easier, and I think you’ll find it very useful in securing a good grade.
      Please do not hesitate with questions, comments, or concerns.
    </p>
  </Card>

  <Card label="FAQ">
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>My score is -1, why?</strong>
        <ol>
          <li>
            The first possibility is that your program has more inputs than expected by the testing framework. For
            instance, you may have added an unexpected loop (the description will be clear if a loop is needed), or you
            may prompt for an additional value. If your program has an extra input, the Codeboard submission will never
            finish and eventually time out, leading to a -1.
          </li>
          <li>
            Second, less likely possibility: there’s a problem with the testing framework. If you are confident you
            have matched the number of inputs in the description, please reach out if you believe there is a problem.
            A -1 grade is a common indicator something isn’t configured properly between Codeboard and Canvas.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The professor sent a note that the testing framework in Codeboard was updated. How can I get the latest
        version?</strong>
        <ol>
          <li>Save all of your files locally. Copy the contents of all of your .cpp and .h files to your local machine.</li>
          <li>
            We’re going to reset your Codeboard project. When you launch the assignment, you should see a prompt about
            “keeping your version” vs. “using the original.” Pick use the original. This will reset your Codeboard
            project to the latest version.
          </li>
          <li>Recreate all of your files and paste in the source.</li>
          <li>Run the tests again with the latest version!</li>
        </ol>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </Card>
</Base>
